  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nExtended period begins with the region locked in a slight westerly\nflow atop a diffuse and washed out frontal boundary and sandwiched\nbetween the Bermuda ridge over FL, upper trof over the great Lakes\nand Berth over TX. This flow regime puts N GA in the perfect\nlocation for moisture to pool and flow parallel to the surface\nboundary...while also allowing weak disturbances to migrate across\nthe region within the weak upper flow. Can see it nicely when\nlooking at RH forecast at 700mb an 850mb.  Model PWAT values \nbeginning Friday suggest daily max values over 2.1 inches possible\nFriday into Saturday, which when combined with a little upper \nforcing and the prospect of training cells suggest flash flooding \npossibilities. The current flood watch extends to Friday night, \nbut in all likelihood will need to be expanded southward and \npossibly extended into Saturday. WPC ERO for Friday puts a portion\nof the I-20 belt in an upper end SLGT risk which correlates \nnicely with the highest moisture plume forecast by model guidance \nat the 700mb and 850mb level. By Saturday, Bertha moves out of the\npicture and a stronger disturbance within the slowly becoming NW\nflow moves toward the area. IF something can organize across KY \nor TN, there could be a low end severe risk, but the flood risk is\nlikely to continue given the continued higher pwat values.\n\nBy Sunday, the disturbance that rolls through on Saturday helps\ndeepen the trof across the eastern US setting up our region in\nfull fledged NW flow. Drier air is forecast to infiltrate the area\nin the mid levels, but there is not any discernible push in the\nlower levels meaning the higher, low to mid 70, dpts will remain\nin place through the extended. Confidence in the Sunday to\nWednesday portion of the forecast is low given the uncertainties\nin dealing with NW flow regimes. NBM unfortunately is yielding sub\n15 pops from Sunday night through Tues despite the higher sfc\ndpts, instabilities and prospect of any NW flow ripple. Certainly\ncant rule out a dry period, but don't have a lot of confidence\ngiven the other factors. Model soundings suggest more dry air in\nthe sounding, but a lack of any real inhibition or cap suggests we\ncould still have some storms around. The other part of the\nperiod...particularly Monday thru Wed is the possibility of a NW\nflow disturbance. ECM and GFS both hinting at something moving\nthrough the flow Tue or Wed which could mean some sort of\nMCS...which in turn increases the severe risk for the area. AI/ML\nguidance also suggest that by day 6 and day 7 some severe probs\nwhich would be expected in this type of setup. For now, the \nextended is a lower confidence forecast and one we will need to \nwatch for both severe and heat hazards.",
